 2. Rolls-Royce&#x27;s expansion 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Rolls-Royce will this week mark the completion of a $1 billion investment in Indiana , where the company has expanded and renovated its manufacturing, assembly and test facilities.

 The big picture: The overhaul competitively positions Rolls-Royce amid wartime-production messaging from the Pentagon and a growing portfolio of projects, including propulsion for the Army's MV-75 , the Air Force's B-52J and the Navy's MQ-25A.

 "If you go back to 2014, we knew that we were at an inflection point. We had aging facilities, some dating back to World War II," Adam Riddle, president of Rolls-Royce Defense, told me. "We didn't have the productivity or the efficiency that we needed, and we knew we had to make a decision," he said. "Are we going to manage a slow decline, or are we going to compete for the future?" Driving the news: Hundreds of people are expected to attend a ribbon-cutting tomorrow. Expected speakers include Indiana Gov. Mike Braun , Adjutant General Maj. Gen. Lawrence Muennich and Indianapolis Mayor Joe Hogsett.

 Follow the money: The $1 billion investment can be divided into two batches:

 $600 million spent modernizing advanced manufacturing, which wrapped in 2021; And $400 million spent on new engine-testing infrastructure. Context: The company has pumped out 14,000-plus engines at its Indianapolis campus since 2001. About half can be traced back to the AE line, seen in the Lockheed Martin C-130J and the Bell-Boeing V-22, among others.

 The bottom line: "We have capacity — right now — to handle increased growth in our core products, increased growth in the programs that we have in development and the programs we think we're going to win," Riddle said.

 "We can do the tests faster. We're doing it more efficiently." Go deeper: Rolls-Royce begins MV-75 engine tests in Indiana 

 
 
