 3. North Korean nuke nuance 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Rebecca Zisser/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump recently blurted out that North Korea has 57 "very powerful" nuclear weapons . 

 Experts told me that is believable, but is most likely an undercount. Why it matters: Nuclear arsenals are often spoken of in generalities — round numbers, increases and decreases, confidence levels. Specifics like this, especially from the very top, are rare and raise hackles.

 Driving the news: Trump's comment, delivered during a press tour of White House construction projects, came shortly after he ordered military drills with South Korea be pared back .

 "Should've never allowed it to happen. They should've never allowed it," Trump said of North Korea's nuclear buildup. "If I were president, I wouldn't have allowed it. But he's got them." By the numbers: The public estimates of Pyongyang's stockpile vary. 

 The Stockholm International Peace Research Institute this summer pegged it "around 60 warheads," with "enough fissile material to produce at least 30 more." What they're saying: The intelligence community "doesn't provide highly specific singular numeric estimates of adversary nuclear forces like this. If Trump heard a number, it was almost certainly a range," Ankit Panda at the Carnegie Endowment for International Peace told me.

 "Among the major uncertainties today is how much of North Korea's weapons-useable nuclear material is actually inside useable and missile-mateable nuclear warheads." What's next: Trump is angling for a meeting this year with North Korea's Kim Jong-un. They last rendezvoused in 2019 .

 
 
